 Only after the argument is coherent does line editing become efficient. Useful passes remove repeated material, replace vague claims with concrete comparisons, shorten descriptions of machinery, and improve the connection between paragraphs. They preserve numbers, units, uncertainty definitions, notation, and attribution. A proposed stylistic change that strengthens a scientific claim is a substantive revision and must be reviewed as such. Repeated generation without a clear diagnosis tends to produce more text instead of a better explanation.
 
+\subsection{Guardrails and guidance}
+\textbf{Bound the writing task before generating prose.} Open-ended writing instructions produce stochastic output: even a request to tighten an argument can change its emphasis or intent. In our experience, this instability increases with the size of the block assigned to the model. This revision supplies a concrete example. A request to tighten this guide produced a comprehensive rewrite, including a new title and abstract, that the human author judged to have changed the intended framing. Restoring the earlier draft and reviewing bounded additions was necessary to recover that intent. This is a documented editing failure, not a measured scaling law for model behavior.
+
+We recommend the following guardrails:
+\begin{itemize}
+\item \textbf{Human-guided structure.} Establish the thesis, audience, outline, and role of each section before drafting. Preserve an accepted title and abstract unless their revision is explicitly requested.
+\item \textbf{Bounded initial drafts.} Assign one subject or argument with stated evidence, exclusions, and intended scope. Ask for an expansion of the outline separately before authorizing a larger draft.
+\item \textbf{Narrow revisions.} Edit section by section, naming the defect to fix and the claims and framing to preserve. An instruction to shorten or clarify is not permission to replace the paper's central argument.
+\item \textbf{Redline validation.} Preserve the accepted version and inspect the changes before accepting each edit. Check additions and deletions for shifts in intent, emphasis, claim strength, citation scope, notation, and evidence. A successful compile checks typesetting; it cannot validate those semantic choices.
+\end{itemize}
+The \texttt{human-directed-science-writing} skill should enforce this bounded workflow. Broader restructuring is a separate proposal for human review. Once a section is accepted, use it as the baseline for the next narrow edit rather than repeatedly regenerating the whole manuscript.
+
 The human author remains responsible for the final document, including passages the agent drafted. The goal is a paper whose claims, priorities, and reasoning the author can explain under questioning. In that sense, writing is also a test of scientific understanding: difficulty explaining the main point may reveal an unsettled interpretation or an unnecessary calculation. Effective assistance improves the argument while preserving this connection between the scientist and the work.
 
